# Crockpot API — Auth Endpoints
#
# Run top-to-bottom, once per session, in VS Code with the REST Client
# extension. See requests/README.md for setup. Register/confirm/resend
# need no token — see CLAUDE.md's ".http" note for why this file starts
# here rather than at CROC-008.
#
# Uses a real Resend send and a real Neon dev DB row — check your inbox
# for the register requests below, and run the Cleanup section at the
# end before re-running this file.
#
# Resend rejects @example.com recipients outright (reserved
# documentation-only domain, RFC 2606) regardless of account/domain
# verification status — replace REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_REAL_EMAIL below with
# a real address you can check before running the ✅/409 sections, which
# are the only two that actually reach Resend (the others fail
# validation first). The password/invalid-email cases stay @example.com
# on purpose — they never reach Resend.


# ══════════════════════════════════════════
#  POST /auth/register
# ══════════════════════════════════════════

### ✅ 201 — fresh registration, sends a real confirmation code by email
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "email_here",
  "password": "correcthorsebattery",
  "name": "HTTP Test User"
}

###

### 🔒 400 — password too short
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "crockpot-http-test-2@example.com",
  "password": "short",
  "name": "HTTP Test User"
}

###

### 🔒 400 — password too long (>72 bytes)
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "crockpot-http-test-3@example.com",
  "password": "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa",
  "name": "HTTP Test User"
}

###

### 🔒 400 — invalid email format
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "not-an-email",
  "password": "correcthorsebattery",
  "name": "HTTP Test User"
}

###

### ✅ 201 — re-registers the same still-unconfirmed email above (case (c):
### abandoned signup). NOT a 409 — clears the old code, issues a new one,
### overwrites the password. A true 409 needs the account confirmed first.
### Subject to the same 60s resend cooldown as /auth/resend-confirmation —
### if run within 60s of the request above, expect 429 resend_too_soon
### instead of 201 (wait, or just treat that as the cooldown demo).
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "email_here",
  "password": "correcthorsebattery",
  "name": "HTTP Test User"
}


# ══════════════════════════════════════════
#  POST /auth/confirm
#
#  Run the register section above first. Two codes get sent to the same
#  inbox (the ✅ 201 and the re-register below it both issue one) — only
#  the SECOND (latest) is still active, the first is dead the moment the
#  second is issued. Paste the latest code into CODE_FROM_EMAIL below.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════

### ✅ 200 — confirms with the real code from the second email above
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/confirm
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "email_here",
  "code": "046196"
}

###

### 🔒 400 — unknown email (never reveals whether the email exists)
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/confirm
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "crockpot-http-test-nobody@example.com",
  "code": "000000"
}

###

### 🔒 400 — wrong code. Run this block 5 times in a row (attempts 1-5,
### each still code_invalid), then a 6th time to see the lockout flip to
### too_many_attempts. Run the ✅ 200 confirm request above again
### afterward to confirm even the *real* code now fails — the
### resend-confirmation section below is what recovers from this.
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/confirm
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "email_here",
  "code": "000000"
}


# ══════════════════════════════════════════
#  POST /auth/resend-confirmation
# ══════════════════════════════════════════

### ✅ 200 — issues a fresh code, sends a real email. Also the recovery
### path after the lockout block above — run this, then re-run the ✅ 200
### confirm request with the newly emailed code.
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/resend-confirmation
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "email_here"
}

###

### 🔒 429 — run the request above twice in a row within 60s to see this
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/resend-confirmation
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "email_here"
}

###

### 🔒 400 — unknown email
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/resend-confirmation
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "crockpot-http-test-nobody@example.com"
}

###

### 🔒 400 — already confirmed (run after actually confirming above)
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/resend-confirmation
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "email_here"
}


# ══════════════════════════════════════════
#  Cleanup
# ══════════════════════════════════════════

### Only the ✅ 201 request actually inserts a row — every other section
### fails before reaching the DB (validation errors, or the 409 case
### re-hitting the same existing row). Connect via psql/a DB client and
### run manually (no DELETE endpoint exists for this):
#
#   DELETE FROM users WHERE email = 'email_here';
#   -- use whatever you actually swapped that placeholder for above
